+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`~datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole number of seconds,
+like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for
+days, `h` for hours, `m` for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may
+appear at most once and in that order, without spaces, so `1h30m` and
+`1d2h3m4s` are valid but `30m1h`, `1h1h`, and `1h 30m` are not. Units are
+case-insensitive. Negative numbers and decimals are rejected, so use `1h30m`
+rather than `1.5h`.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=[])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6
+```
+
